{"type":"after","callId":"call@1080","endTime":431760.146,"result":{"matches":true,"received":{"value":{"s":"visible"},"ariaSnapshot":"- heading \"Crossing investigation\" [level=2]\n- text: NGSS MS-ETS1 · HS-ETS1 · HS-PS2\n- strong: \"Status:\"\n- text: ✓ SAFE — governing safety factor 17.90 (Euler buckling; both modeled checks ≥ 2.0) Design parameters\n- table:\n  - rowgroup\n- text: Stress and buckling analysis\n- table:\n  - rowgroup\n- text: Quantities — one planar truss\n- table:\n  - rowgroup\n- heading \"Current design reasoning\" [level=3]\n- paragraph:\n  - strong: \"Prediction:\"\n  - text: I predict that a thicker section will improve the buckling margin because bending stiffness increases.\n- paragraph:\n  - strong: \"Observation:\"\n  - text: The whole crossing met the target. A diagonal changed from tension to compression as the vehicle moved.\n- heading \"Current design crossing test\" [level=3]\n- paragraph: Lowest safety factor 2.15 at 8.3% of span; buckling governs at DL0.\n- paragraph: 57 static vehicle positions tested with 120 kN. This records the crossing test separately from the current-position analysis above. Vehicle dynamics and deflection are not checked.\n- heading \"Saved design comparison\" [level=3]\n- paragraph: Saved inputs are recalculated with the current classroom model. Material cost and mass cover one planar truss. Each vehicle safety factor describes the saved position; completed crossing evidence is listed separately.\n- article:\n  - heading \"Crossing investigation\" [level=4]\n  - table\n  - paragraph\n  - paragraph\n- strong: \"Honest limit of this analysis:\"\n- text: \"Member forces are solved for an ideal planar, pin-jointed truss. Yield and Euler buckling use solid square sections and pinned ends. Quantities cover one planar truss and exclude the deck, second truss, lateral-bracing members, connections, and waste. Material dead weight is not added to the applied load. Vehicle results describe only the current position unless an explicitly labeled crossing test is included. Deflection, fatigue, dynamics, and connection capacity are not checked. This classroom model is for learning, not construction. Sources: NGSS Lead States (2013) · Hibbeler, R.C. (2017), Structural Analysis · AASHTO LRFD Bridge Design Specifications · Petroski, H. (1994), Design Paradigms (Tacoma Narrows + Tay Bridge + Hyatt). Printed from AlloFlow STEAM Lab.\""}},"afterSnapshot":"after@call@1080"}
